# The Hidden Trap in `contains` with Nested Collections

A common stumbling block in Laravel development occurs when developers assume that `contains` operates on the top-level keys of a collection, only to discover it returns `false` even when the target value clearly exists within the data structure. In your specific case, you have a collection containing a single item (index 0), and that item is an array with three nested arrays inside it. Your loop iterates over `$this->options`, extracting each option's ID, but the check `dd($options->contains($option->id))` fails to fire.

The root cause lies in understanding what `contains` actually checks. The method looks for the value at the first level of the collection. In your dump output, `$options` is a Collection where the first-level keys are indices (0, 1, 2). The actual data resides inside the array found at index 0. When you call `dd($options)`, you see that structure clearly:

```php
Collection {#390
  #items: array:1 [
    0 => array:3 [ // This is the top-level item of the collection
        0 => array:7 ["id" => 10, ...]
        1 => array:7 ["id" => 11, ...]
        2 => array:7 ["id" => 12, ...]
    ]
  ]
}
```

Here, `$options->contains(10)` checks if the value `10` exists as a key or value in the top-level array of items. Since the top-level array only has one item (at key `0`), and that item is not `10`, the method returns false. The ID `10` is buried two levels deep: inside the collection item, which is itself an array containing another array.

To fix this, you must either flatten the data structure before checking or modify your logic to navigate into the nested arrays. If your intention is to verify if any of the nested options have a specific ID, you should iterate through the nested arrays within your option or use `contains` on the flattened list of IDs.

Here is how you can refactor the loop to correctly check for existence:

```php
foreach ($this->options as $option) {
    // Assuming $option is the array at index 0 of the collection item
    // We need to check inside $option[0], $option[1], etc.

    $ids = [];
    foreach ($option as $nestedOption) {
        if (isset($nestedOption['id'])) {
            $ids[] = $nestedOption['id'];
        }
    }

    if ($options->contains($ids, 'value')) {
        // This approach is complex because contains expects a single value or key existence
        // A better pattern is to check the flattened array directly
    }
}
```

A cleaner approach involves extracting all IDs into a new collection and then checking against that. First, map the nested structures to just their IDs:

```php
$allIds = collect($this->options)->flatten(2)->map(function ($item) {
    return $item['id'] ?? null;
});

if ($allIds->contains($option->id)) {
    dd('test'); // This will now work as expected
}
```

Alternatively, if you prefer not to flatten the entire collection and want to keep the logic inside the loop, you can check directly against the nested arrays:

```php
foreach ($this->options as $option) {
    foreach ($option as $nestedItem) {
        if ($nestedItem['id'] === $option->id) {
            dd('test');
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

However, the most efficient solution in Laravel is often to ensure your data structure matches the expectations of the method you are using. If `$this->options` represents a list where each element has an `id` property, consider restructuring your collection so that each item in the top-level array is the nested option itself, rather than wrapping them in an extra array:

```php
// Desired structure for contains to work natively
$cleanOptions = collect($this->options)->flatten(1);

foreach ($this->options as $option) {
    if ($cleanOptions->contains('id', $option->id)) {
        dd('test');
    }
}
```

It is important to note that `contains` accepts a key and a value. In older Laravel versions, the syntax might differ slightly regarding how you pass the key, but generally, passing the second argument as `'key' => $value` allows checking if an item with that specific key holds the provided value.

Always verify your data structure using `dd()` or `var_dump()`. Seeing exactly where your values live prevents assumptions about collection depth. If you are dealing with deeply nested arrays, consider flattening them with `flatten(2)` to simplify subsequent queries. This technique ensures that `contains` operates on a list of scalar values rather than complex associative arrays.
